Expressing viewpoints | A viewpoint needs a clear claim and relevant support. A respectful response may agree partly or disagree with an idea while acknowledging the other speaker. | “I agree that reading time helps, but students need a choice of books” both responds and adds a condition. | Write one sentence partly agreeing with a proposal for longer library hours. | A good sentence names the proposal and adds a specific consideration, such as staffing or access. | A student committee discusses two ways to run a book exchange. | Write a short dialogue with a claim, evidence, and respectful counterpoint. | Speakers address ideas and give reasons rather than judging each other's character.
Cause, effect, and evidence | Cause-and-effect language connects events, but a sequence alone does not establish causation. Use because, therefore, and however only when the relation is supported. | “Visits rose after the poster appeared” reports sequence; saying the poster caused all the rise needs stronger evidence. | Rewrite the claim as a cautious statement that fits the observed sequence. | “Visits rose after the poster appeared, though other factors may have contributed.” | A school report shows changing attendance and weather on the same days. | Write two questions that test alternative explanations. | Ask about schedule changes and the number of participants, not only weather.
Exposition and counterargument | Exposition states a position, supporting reasons, and conclusion. A counterargument deserves a fair summary before it is answered. | An essay for bicycle parking can address cost and safety rather than calling opponents careless. | State one counterargument to adding bicycle parking and a respectful response. | A concern about space can be answered with a smaller trial area and a safety check. | The student council writes an opinion piece on shared study spaces. | Outline the claim, two reasons, counterargument, and measured conclusion. | Each part connects logically, and uncertain data are not presented as certain.
Narrative perspective | Narrative perspective shapes what readers know. First-person narrators may be mistaken or incomplete; compare words and actions before treating their view as fact. | “I knew everyone agreed” may be unreliable if other characters' dialogue shows doubt. | What detail would challenge the narrator's claim? | A line in which another character objects or stays silent with a stated reason would challenge it. | Write one scene twice, from two students' perspectives, about a missing poster. | Keep the event consistent but vary what each narrator knows. | The scenes show different limited knowledge without changing established facts.
Formal letters and applications | Formal letters identify purpose, recipient, relevant details, and an appropriate closing. Claims about experience should be truthful and supported when requested. | A request to use the library names the date, time, activity, and contact route rather than vague phrases. | Write a polite opening sentence requesting a room for a study club. | “Dear Librarian, I am writing to request the reading room for our club meeting on Friday.” | A club asks for permission to hold a public workshop at school. | Draft a brief letter with purpose, schedule, safety plan, and closing. | The recipient can understand and evaluate the request without guessing essential details.
Research and oral presentation | A research presentation gives a question, method, key findings, limitations, and sources. Slides support the speaker and must not hide data origin. | A chart comparing two months needs labels, counts, and a note if the number of school days differs. | Name two elements that make a chart understandable to an audience. | Axis labels with units and a source or time period are essential. | Your group presents a small survey of reading preferences with anonymous responses. | Plan three slides and one answer to a limitation question. | Slides state the question, data and method, then conclusions limited to the surveyed group.
